    "representation": "pdf-text-entry",
    "text": "We say that two subsets A, B of an infinite group G are separated if there\nexists an infinite subset X of G such that 1 ∈ X, X = X −1 , and XAX ∩ B = ∅. Is\nit true that any two disjoint subsets A, B of an infinite group G satisfying |A| < |G|,\n|B| < |G| are separated? This is so if A, B are finite, or G is Abelian. I. V. Protasov",
